WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:16.219
EVA B: I grew up
in Czechoslovakia.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:20.030
I went to school there, and
lived there through my teens.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:20.930
In Prague.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.380
In the city, in the center
of the heart of the city,

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.350
in the old city.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:25.340
I was an only child.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.710
My father died before
the war, so it was just

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:28.800
my mother and me.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.160
Oh, I had two girl
cousins that--

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.140
you know, we had one--
we lived in the city,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.550
and we had an
apartment upstairs.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.320
And these two girls had--

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.558
and this was my mother's
brother, and his wife.

00:00:38.558 --> 00:00:40.100
And these two girls
lived downstairs,

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.660
and they were
practically my sisters.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.280
You know, we did
everything together.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.380
I more or less lived with them,
and they didn't come back.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.450
They were younger than I was.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.140
And I had kind of a high
school sweetheart, a boyfriend,

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.870
whom I had had for
five years or six years

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.020
by the time I went to camp.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.240
And he was a highly
gifted individual, musical

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.410
and academically, and
he did everything.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.570
He didn't come back.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:12.200
Those were the things that
hurt, is my, personal.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.590
Of course my mother never got
over the loss of her siblings,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:16.940
she really didn't.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.340
I'm not aware of there
being much discrimination

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.750
against Jews before the war.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:25.690
It was pretty open and
integrated society.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:30.410
I remember some cat-calling
of names on the street once.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.620
But in my-- I was
in a public school.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.700
And, uh, was a large
Jewish minority there.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.620
And there was an influx
of German immigrants,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.230
and they kind of brought
the fear with them, which--

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.580
until-- until '39, Czechs
were saying, "Oh, it's

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:46.920
not going to happen here.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.310
Nothing is going to happen here.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:48.990
We are safe.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:49.900
We are all right."

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.160
We still were kidding
ourselves that it's safe.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.090
But then in --

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.850
the Germans marched
in on March 15, '39.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.070
That was kind of a
shock to the system.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.460
I remember that
day very vividly.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.360
I-- I don't know why.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.090
I went to the post office,
and it was sleeting.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.350
And on a corner of the street
that was standing a-- a--

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:14.017
a Czech policeman directing
traffic and the German soldier

00:02:14.017 --> 00:02:15.100
next to him who took over.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.600
And the Czech
policeman stood there,

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.140
and he didn't know what to do,
because the German had taken

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.490
over directing the traffic.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.430
And it was kind of a
really classic illustration

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.910
of the conflict of power, the
two of them standing there,

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.286
and the German taking over.

00:02:30.286 --> 00:02:32.770
We had had no warning at all.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:34.840
My mother started
talking about emigration

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.370
and took me out of
the public school

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.120
and put me into a
private language school,

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.280
because she wanted me
to learn English fast.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:50.880
And so-- and afterwards, I was
kind of apprenticed in a trade

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.760
because I was supposed
to have something

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.880
I could do when I came over
to wherever you were going.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.660
And then first, the public
schools were closed to Jews.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.370
We weren't allowed
into public schools.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.840
Then we weren't allowed
into restaurants.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.340
Then we weren't
allowed into parks.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.740
Then we weren't
allowed into schools.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.100
Then the-- this little
workshop I was working was,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.260
you had to have special
restrooms for Jews.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.710
And of course, this was like
a workshop with 10 people.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.810
And this was impossible
for him, the man,

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:20.600
to comply with this
kind of demand.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.542
So he had to let me go.

00:03:22.542 --> 00:03:25.370
So by that time, I was
realizing that things

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.620
were different for me.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.670
October '42, we were
called up by a notice

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.820
to appear in the fair grounds.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.387
There was a fair
there, grounds there,

00:03:37.387 --> 00:03:39.470
in-- building, a big
building, a covered building,

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.930
and to assemble for transport.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.290
My mother and I went from
Prague to Theresienstadt

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.340
in Czechoslovakia.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:47.600
And that was still--

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.260
within Czechoslovakia.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.760
And I guess we were lucky that
we stayed there for two years.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.688
And uh-- I was working,
and my mother was working.

00:03:58.688 --> 00:03:59.730
And we had extra rations.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.770
So we did-- we we lived OK.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.710
We also had a couple
of parcels from home,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.950
from some family
that were not Jewish.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.760
In a room about the size
of this one, I'd say 12--

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.260
it could have been
a little bigger--

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.070
12 by 8-- 18, it was longer--

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:17.610
we were in three-layered
ba-- bunks,

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:20.339
and there were 35
people in that room.

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.998
It was a little old
house, and there was--

00:04:22.998 --> 00:04:24.540
the bathrooms were--
the horror there

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.420
was just two kind of outhouses
on the-- in the hallway

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.790
to serve the whole house
that was equally full.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:35.100
And one year, the Red
Cross Commission came,

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.500
and they emptied
out all the rooms

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.000
that were at street
level, you know,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.390
and put one couple in each room,
and moved every 35 people--

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:45.810
relatively, depending on
the size of the room-- out,

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.270
and just jammed us up
more, so that they should--

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:53.310
could show the Red Cross
how humane this place was.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.540
They were flanked by--

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.300
by Germans, and, you
know, escorted through

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.210
and shown around.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:01.680
And the streets they were taken
through had been cleaned up

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.730
and the houses painted.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.230
And on the ground
floor level, there

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:05.490
was one couple in each room.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:12.460
And they showed the Red Cross
this lovely, um, settlement

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.245
that was so clean and nice.

00:05:14.245 --> 00:05:15.745
They were never
left by the Germans,

00:05:15.745 --> 00:05:18.430
so there was no
way to get to them.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:20.420
I must say, that
while I and my mother,

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.387
we were both young and
healthy at the time.

00:05:22.387 --> 00:05:23.470
We were quite comfortable.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.560
A lot of people died and
just from undernourishment

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.460
and stress, and especially
the older people.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:33.860
I mean, the amount
of food that seemed

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.020
to be sufficient for
me or my mother-- also

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:37.220
we had some extras.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:41.960
The old people died, I mean,
in the hundreds and thousands.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.360
Well, there was a call up again.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.260
It was 2,658 people.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.060
I know, because I was
in the last carriage,

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.730
and this was numbered,
and everybody was counted.

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:55.380
And we were in cattle wagons.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.180
They were covered at that time.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.480
I mean, they-- they had slats,
but there was a roof over them.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.450
And this was in October '44.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.130
And we went to Auschwitz.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:08.940
It took two days, 48 hours
or better, to get there.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.140
Well, we had two buckets.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.960
One was water, and
one for other needs.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.110
And there were two children
in the car with us,

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:19.860
one like a four-year-old,
and a six-year-old.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.660
And then there was
an infant in the car.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:26.036
And those kids were so good,
it was absolutely amazing.

00:06:26.036 --> 00:06:30.290
Uh, I-- I don't know.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:36.430
It was-- I suppose I managed to
really kind of insulate myself,

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:37.090
and say I--

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:39.540
I wasn't really
participating in all that.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.140
I built a wall
around me and said,

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:42.730
that's not happening to me.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:46.870
I-- It can't be true, and I'm
not participating in this.

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.100
And I just really
shut myself off.

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.790
And that's how I went
through the whole war.

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.310
And then when we arrived in
Auschwitz in the evening,

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:59.530
we could see this rather
ghostly landscape, these flash--

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.080
flood lights on the
barbed wire, which went on

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:03.670
for miles and miles and miles.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:05.380
It all you could see.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:08.220
I just remember when we got
out of the train in Auschwitz

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:12.160
that we were being separated
right on the platform,

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.155
as you stepped out of the
train, and sent left and right.

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:20.870
And they wanted to
separate me and my mother,

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.030
and my mother said, we
are mother and daughter,

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:24.830
leave us together.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:27.140
So he motioned us to
one side together.

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:28.880
We heard that whoever
wasn't with us--

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.690
there was 200 women where
we were in the morning--

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:32.300
was dead.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:38.080
They all had gone straight to
the gas oven, 2,456 people.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.070
We were told right the
minute we were separated

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:42.830
and sent over to that
one side that this

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:45.820
was all that was left of
that train load of people.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.440
And amongst this group
was my girlfriend,

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:50.690
whose mother had gone
to the other side,

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:52.520
and was my gym
teacher, whose children

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.590
had been sent to the other side.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:57.930
That was the first time I
ever heard about gas chambers.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:01.620
I mean, it was just too big
and too shocking to absorb.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:03.780
My mother started
crying, but I still

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:05.590
somehow refused to believe it.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:07.260
I just didn't.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:11.460
I suppose in a way she knew that
she had just narrowly escaped.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.495
But I just-- it was
too monstrous, too big.

00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:17.800
It was unbelievable.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:19.680
There was a very famous
clarinetist with us,

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:20.463
too in that train.

00:08:20.463 --> 00:08:21.630
And he had an eye infection.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.740
He wore dark glasses,
so he went to the gas.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:26.290
They kept us in
that one hall, which

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:28.790
was cement floors and
nothing else, all day.

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:32.299
Afterwards they asked us to
strip, and we were shaved.

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:34.919
I mean, our heads
and body were shaved.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:36.650
And we were herded through--

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.600
past soldiers, so now
I was, like, 13 or 14,

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.810
and I was walking naked past
soldiers all the time, which--

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:50.340
really was-- after the first
day or so, you get used to it.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:55.175
But, um-- and then we were
herded into a bathhouse

00:08:55.175 --> 00:08:56.380
with overhead showers.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:59.050
By that time, we knew that the
gas chambers were dressed up

00:08:59.050 --> 00:08:59.770
as shower rooms.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:02.140
And we decided, there
were holes in the windows.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:03.130
The glass was broken.

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:06.510
This wasn't a gas chamber.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:09.750
So that's um--

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.460
and then we were
herded into barracks.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:16.320
Barracks were long, big, wooden
kind of Quonset huts with--

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.450
with planks that people would--
we were lying six on one plank.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:22.950
And then one person
wanted to turn.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:25.380
We had six of us to turn,
because there wasn't

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.090
any room for anything else.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:28.860
The worst part
for me at the time

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.050
was, we had-- we had
roll call every morning.

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:35.217
This was in Poland in October,
and Poland in October is cold,

00:09:35.217 --> 00:09:36.550
and we didn't have much clothes.

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:39.300
They had issues us-- they
didn't issue us uniforms.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:41.490
Uniforms are solid
and protective.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:43.560
We had just been given old rags.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:47.040
And we would stand on roll call
from 6 o'clock in the morning

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.560
till 11 o'clock at noon,
or something like that,

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.140
until every head in the
camp was accounted for.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:54.840
And this was like 100,000, and
the roll call would never work.

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:56.920
And they come back
and count again.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.560
And-- and in the
evening again, this

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.990
was way before dark
till way into daylight,

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.440
and in the evening again
for three, four, five hours,

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.910
we would be standing
for roll call.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:08.370
And for the first three
or four days in Auschwitz,

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.860
we never got anything to eat.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:15.850
After fourth day, somebody
complained to one of the kapos.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:18.960
And evidently, we
should have been issued

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:20.720
rations, which hadn't happened.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.160
So she got us some soup.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.040
That was the only food I got
in six days in Auschwitz,

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.150
was one soup.

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:30.360
We-- we stayed together, the
group of 200 of us who had been

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.091
selected from the--

00:10:32.091 --> 00:10:35.550
from that transport, and we
were all young, healthy people.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.410
I mean, we had been
selected once over again

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:38.702
when we were in the bath house.

00:10:38.702 --> 00:10:40.653
We had been selected naked.

00:10:40.653 --> 00:10:41.570
You know, that's true.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:44.070
And again, they wanted to
send my mother off, and they--

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:46.427
and then they asked her
whether she had somebody there,

00:10:46.427 --> 00:10:47.760
and she said she had a daughter.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:48.550
They let her pass.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:51.180
There were about three women
at her age in the group.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:53.850
The rest of us was
all very young.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.130
I actually was in
Auschwitz only one week,

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:57.600
and then I was
shipped off again.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.550
The last day, we
were standing outside

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:03.240
from before dawn in the
morning to dark at night,

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.000
and I just didn't think
I could stand anymore.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.220
It was really-- my
back was playing up,

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:11.490
and we kind of huddled together
and supported each other,

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:14.200
because we really
couldn't stand.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.330
And I-- then we were
given a piece of bread,

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:21.450
and a piece of margarine, and
a piece of salami for the trip.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:23.640
And there was-- it was
given to us like that.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.800
Nothing to hold it
in, or keep it in.

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.580
So the bread we kept, but
the margarine and the meat

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.360
I ate, and I got very sick.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:33.390
Threw up immediately.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.540
And I also arrived in
the camp in-- in Germany

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.250
with pretty high temperature.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:40.050
I had a reaction.

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:41.750
And they had kind-- we were--

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:45.840
well, the trip was quite
something else again.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:47.508
This was now in--

00:11:47.508 --> 00:11:48.800
no, this was still cattle cars.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.850
This was in, still,
the open cars.

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.180
I was beginning to doubt
that I would make it,

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:55.920
'cause I simply
couldn't stand anymore,

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:58.380
and I couldn't see how I was
going to get out of this.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:02.520
And I guess by that time, I
already had the temperature.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.330
I was sick too.

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:06.900
I arrived in Germany with
a high of 42 degrees, which

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:07.800
I don't know what--

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:10.020
Celsius, which is quite a bit.

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:15.450
There was a small factory, and
we had dry and heated sleeping

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:15.960
quarters.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.880
That was big advantage.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:21.960
They had-- had to evacuate
this labor or this work camp

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.100
where we were in.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.170
This was April '45.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.660
And they returned us
to Theresienstadt.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.090
They had sent
thousands and thousands

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:35.840
of people from all parts of
Germany back to Theresienstadt.

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:37.930
And as a matter of fact,
that was the first time I

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:39.970
saw these photographs, these--

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:42.103
you know, you see
this piles of corpses,

00:12:42.103 --> 00:12:44.020
the photographs of the
pile of corpses I think

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:45.293
everybody has seen by now.

00:12:45.293 --> 00:12:46.960
But I think the much
more horrible thing

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:49.450
is to see these corpses walk.

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:51.340
And that was for the
first time I saw them.

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:55.330
Walking into this typhoid
and cholera-ridden place

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:57.520
was liberation for me.

00:12:57.520 --> 00:12:59.473
And then they wanted
us to stay there.

00:12:59.473 --> 00:13:01.390
And we were healthy, you
know, and the chances

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:04.640
that we'd catch something
there were really pretty good.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:07.160
So we got Red Cross
parcels in there.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:11.110
We, joined a laundry cart
that was taking laundry

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.570
out of the camp, and smuggled
our way out of the camp,

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:14.740
and went back to Prague.

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:17.690
And we went into a hotel,
and we got two wide beds,

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:20.500
with sheets, and
linens, and blankets.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:24.620
And there was hot and warm
and running water in the room.

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:28.160
And I-- I didn't know
it could feel that way.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.210
And we both got sick.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:32.130
We both were in bed
with a high temperature.

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:35.090
And there was no public
transport, and nobody to call,

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:35.940
and no food.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:39.680
And so then we
remembered somebody

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.990
from the friends we
had at the end of the--

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:45.890
and-- with my temperature,
I picked myself up

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:49.100
and walked over there, because
my mother was sicker than I.

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:51.860
And she gave us tea and toast.

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:54.530
That was liberation.

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.570
The whole extent of
what had happened

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:59.270
didn't hit us till
after the war.

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:02.900
I mean, like, out-- out of
my mother's family, out of--

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:04.650
there's a-- it's strange.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:06.750
There were 13 people
in my mother's family,

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:08.320
and all she had was married--

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:12.360
four married sisters, and two
brothers with their daughters,

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:14.350
and sons and wives and husbands.

00:14:14.350 --> 00:14:18.240
13 went into camp,
nobody came back.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:19.950
And I had a group
of young people.

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:25.590
We used to meet in
Prague just before camps.

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:27.110
Social group.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:30.440
And out of the-- all my age,
and out of this group of 13,

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:32.160
one came back.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:33.210
I just pushed it away.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:34.880
I decided I couldn't
think about it.

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:35.880
I couldn't cope with it.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:39.270
And I think it's no
more than five years

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:41.560
now that I can even touch on it.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:43.470
And I really was
so busy adjusting

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:46.560
and repressing and living,
and I just didn't want to.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:47.650
I never told anybody.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:48.150
I--

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:51.000
when I met my husband, who
was also from Czechoslovakia,

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.870
and spent his four
years in Japanese camp,

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:58.020
we did exchange information.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.360
But I think that's the only
time I ever mentioned it.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:01.920
I never mentioned
it to my children.

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:05.650
They never-- I never
talked to them about it.

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:10.280
They practically don't
know that I was in a camp.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:11.140
What stayed with me?

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:12.280
The defensiveness.

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:15.970
The not wanting to feel,
not wanting to be involved.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:20.850
What I'm very aware now is,
of course, the lack of family.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:24.130
And I-- that I have
been always aware of.

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:25.880
I mean, ever since I
came to this country,

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:30.020
I have this feeling of
being a waif, of being alone

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:33.210
in the world, which I am.

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:36.730
My husband lost his parents.

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:38.860
He had one brother
who has died since.

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:47.800
Of course, my husband died,
and I have no family left.

